Z||000000||rogergarrison||03-26-2001||06:55 AM||||Gotta love Tony Stewart. Hes not afraid to show his 'feelings' Did you hear the crowd cheer his 'bump n run' to Jeff Gordon!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||03-26-2001||09:23 AM||||Yeah, I bet Tony's crew is real excited.  They've gotta mount a new fascia, bump the fender and fix all those bent support brackets.  Hehe, takes nothing away from what Gordon did, he was under the yellow line and should've backed off.  BTW, this wasn't the first time Gordon has spun someone in the closing laps at Bristol.<P>Ohhh.. didn't Stewart and Gordon had a shoving match off the track last year?  Or was it Robbie Gordon I'm thinking. <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||03-26-2001||10:32 AM||||Now I'm no particular fan of Gordon or Stewart.. but you need to watch the reruns. Gordon was clean. His front wheel was up by Stewart's rear wheel. He had the inside. Stewart started coming down on him and Jeff went all the way down inside the yellow line till he couldn't go any more. Stewart tried something and it didn't work...<P>I loved his little retaliation move although it was unfounded.. Made for some good viewing. But his immediate whining to NASCAR was pretty childish. Looks like Gordon will lose his role as Whiner Boy this year.<P>By the way.. My boy is leading the points again! Even if the car is not quite as pretty, still gotta love that 88!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-26-2001||01:20 PM||||I raced myself years ago in Nascar. The gentlemans rule is yield to a guy if his fender is up to your door, not up to the back wheel-so Tony had every rite to cut Gordon off. It would have been better though if they had left each other space to run.<P>btw, it was Tony and Gordon who have had a few bumps between them. Most memorable was Watkins Glen last year. Im sure Tony will get at least a hefty fine for the pit road, but as far as the on track thing, Tony didnt do any thing wrong, Gordon should have backed off. They rebuild the total car every week anyway and that same car may not be used for next 20 races, so its no big deal to crew.(about like me having 10 cars to drive and one has a flat tire)<P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-26-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||03-27-2001||11:53 AM||||Tony was fined $10,000 and is on probation until Aug 29, next 18 races.  Gordon was found to be blameless and not fined.  Really, the accident was just "one of those racin deals."<P>Stewart did appologize afterward.<P>And yes, it was at Michigan last year when the two had tangled on the track which led to the garage.  I think I remember the Watkins Glen incident too.<P>Yea, I know it's really not a big thing for Tony's crew to fix the damage.  I do feel it kinda shows a little disrespect for his car, team, other drivers and teams, and maybe Home Depot (They probably don't care anyway because the car is getting air time on RPM tonight).<P>Maybe it's just me, but I don't think running into another car on pit road is a constructive way of venting, although it might feel good.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-27-2001).]||||reg||1
